(defmodule session
  "**One event vocabulary for every evaluation session the editor keeps.**

bedit runs several `std/editor/evalsession` children at once — the Brood eval sandbox,
the Elixir agent the playground and `*BEAM Processes*` share, the `MIX_ENV=test` node
`C-c t` runs suites in — and more will follow (a Ruby session, a Python one; each is a
backend and nothing else).

Each of those is a BACKEND: how to spawn its child, how to put a request on its wire, how
to take a reply off it. None of them is an event vocabulary, and for a while all three
carried one anyway — three `-ready-event` / `-reply-event` / `-down-event` records, three
`emit` functions casing over the same three kinds, three sets of `UiEvent` impls — for the
sole purpose of letting the router tell whose event it was holding.

That fact is one the session already knows about itself, so it travels on the event:
`evalsession` hands its `:emit` hook the session's registered NAME, and the three records
below carry it. One emit ([[session-emit]]) serves every backend, one set of `UiEvent`
impls (in `sandbox-events`) folds every session's events, and a new language is a spec
plus its own verbs — which is all a backend ever should have been.

`ready` carries the child's whole BANNER as well as the generation. A Brood sandbox's
banner says only that it is up; the Elixir agent's says which application started and
which Elixir it is running. Clients that do not care ignore the argument; the ones that
do could not be told any other way, because only the child knows."
  (:use model)) ; UiEvent — these three records are folded by the loop
;; `editor/evalsession` is referenced qualified below (the `mod/name` reference loads it).

;; ---- the events ----------------------------------------------------------------
;; `:session` is the registered name of the worker the event came from (`:sandbox`,
;; `:elixir-sandbox`, `:elixir-test`, …) — what scopes every fold in `sandbox-events`.
;; Without it a `down` from one child reads as a `down` from another, which in a mix
;; project means respawning a VM that recompiles the world because a different language's
;; session died.

(defrecord session-ready (session gen banner)) ; the child announced itself; the session is empty
(defrecord session-reply (session reply gen)) ; one decoded reply map
(defrecord session-down (session code gen)) ; the child went away (a respawn usually follows)
(defn session-emit (sink kind payload gen name)
  "`evalsession`'s `:emit` hook for every backend in this editor — turn a session event
into the `UiEvent` record the `ui-run` loop folds and send it to `sink` — the loop that started the
session, or the one whose request this answers (brood `evalsession` routes by request).

The one place the supervision touches the editor, which is what lets the session itself
stay in `std` knowing nothing about a loop, a model or a frame. A backend names this
symbol in its spec and writes no event code at all."
  (let (target (or sink :editor))
    (case kind
      :ready (send target (session-ready name gen payload))
      :reply (send target (session-reply name payload gen))
      :down (send target (session-down name payload gen))
      nil)))

(defn forget-script (path)
  "Remove the temporary program a backend spat for its child, once the child has read it.

Every session here is started by writing a script to an unguessable path and pointing a
runtime at it, and the ready banner is the proof the file has done its job. Best-effort on
purpose: a respawn re-writes it, and failing to delete costs a stray file rather than a
session."
  (try
    (when (and path (file/exists? path)) (file/rm path))
    (catch e (log/warn (str "session: could not remove " path ": " (error-message e))))))

(defn emit-after-script (path sink kind payload gen name)
  "[[session-emit]], preceded by forgetting the child's script on the ready banner — the
`:emit` hook for a backend that spits one. The whole of what those three backends' own
emits were once the event vocabulary stopped being per-session."
  (when (= kind :ready) (forget-script path))
  (session-emit sink kind payload gen name))

;; ---- the client API ------------------------------------------------------------
;; Thin over `evalsession`, and thin on purpose: what these add is that a caller names a
;; SESSION rather than repeating the shape of one. A backend module keeps only the verbs
;; that are its own — `sandbox-check`, `elixir-observe`, `elixir-attach`, `test-run` — and
;; the four below stop being written out per language.

(defn running? (name)
  "Is the session's worker alive?"
  (editor/evalsession/session-running? name))

(defn start (name spec &optional (root nil) (sink nil))
  "Ensure session `name` is up with backend `spec`, its child rooted in `root`. Idempotent;
a session already running elsewhere is re-rooted. Answers its pid.

Call it as early as a feature knows it will want one: a runtime's startup — in a mix
project, a whole compile — then happens while the reader is still reading.

The session's lifecycle (ready, down) goes to `sink`, by default the LOOP this is running in
(`*ui-loop*`) — else `:editor`, for setup done before a loop starts. Not `:editor` alone: that
is the first frame, and a second frame or a `--serve` client that started the session would
never hear it had restarted. A session already running subscribes the caller beside whoever
started it."
  (editor/evalsession/session-start name spec root (or sink *ui-loop* :editor)))

(defn request (name id timeout-ms payload)
  "Fire-and-forget: put request `id` on session `name`'s wire. Answers whether it was
ACCEPTED — false means the session is not running and no reply for this id will ever
arrive, which the caller must not record as in flight.

The answer comes back to the LOOP that asked (`*ui-loop*`), so a playground in a second frame
gets its own answers rather than the first frame's mailbox getting them (brood's
`evalsession` routes by request). Asked outside a loop, it goes to every subscriber."
  (editor/evalsession/session-request name id timeout-ms payload *ui-loop*))

(defn reset (name)
  "Restart the session: a fresh child, an empty session. In a project that compiles this is
a real pause, which is why it is a command someone asks for and never something the editor
does on its own."
  (editor/evalsession/session-reset name))

(defn shutdown (name)
  "Kill the child and stop the worker. A no-op when it is not running."
  (editor/evalsession/session-shutdown name))
